<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.2.1: Perfect Squares Revisited</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01249</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.2.1: Perfect Squares Revisited</md:title>
    <md:uuid>deaf5f45-a0d4-4465-b291-bd0f959f6ecb</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>This warm up reviews perfect squares and square roots and begins to elicit the idea of higher powers and the nth root.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of 2.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(4 \times 4\)? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 16
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(4^2\)? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 16
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Using what you know about roots, what is the value of \(\sqrt{16}\)? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 4
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Talk to a partner. How can you prove \(\sqrt[2]{4^2}\;=\;4\)? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Since \(4^2\;=\;4\;\cdot\;4\;=\;16\) and \(\sqrt{16}\;=\;4\), \(\sqrt[2]{4^2}\;=\;4\).
    <para>Notice that the root and exponents are the same and simplify to leave the integer only.</para>
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> Talk to a partner. What do you think the answer to \(\sqrt[5]{4^5}\) would be? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\sqrt[5]{4^5}\;=\;4\)
  </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>If time allows, continue to give more examples where the exponent and index of the root are the same. Ask students to come up with a rule or pattern.</para></section>
</content>
